      "text": "To find the smallest four-digit number that is divisible by 33, we must first find the smallest four-digit number. The smallest four-digit number is 1000.\n\nNow, we must find the remainder when 1000 is divided by 33. We can do this by finding the remainder of the integral divisions of 1000 by successive multiples of 33. Since we're working with integers, we can exclude a remainder of 26 because 1000 was found prior to 33 times 30 + 26.\n\nThere are a total of 30 integers between 0 and 1000 that when divided by 33 will leave no remainder.\n\nNow, we will find the remainder of all of these divisions and look for the remainder of 25 because 26 was excluded previously.\n\nWe can perform the division of 130 by 33 first, which illustrates the remainder of 25 until we reach 1000.\n\nTherefore, the smallest four-digit number, 1000, can be divided by 33 with a remainder of 25.\n\nTo find the smallest four-digit number divisible by 33, just subtract the remainder's number from 1000 and get your resulting answer (1000 - 25).\n\n\nTherefore, the smallest four-digit number divisible by 33 is $\\boxed{1000 - 25, 975}$.\n\nTo show that $0 \\le 25 < 975$ is true.\nTo be certain, I tried to include a $0$ at the end instead of a $5$. Inevitably, it wasn't a four-digit number.",
